_:n86cca99ae73d49aaa5f925a6ed7fb48cb8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#first> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01284#animalization_scene> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The scene depicts a person wearing a pig mask and holding a small pig figurine, symbolizing animalization and dehumanization. The person is positioned in front of a cafe window, with the text overlay 'muslims offend me' reinforcing a harmful stereotype. The visual elements, including the pig mask and figurine, are directly mapped onto the text to create a hateful association."@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#TextualMaterial> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01284#toxicity_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The meme directly attacks Muslims based on a harmful stereotype equating them with pigs, using a pig mask and figurine to dehumanize them. This constitutes a hateful attack targeting a protected characteristic (religion)."@en .
_:n86cca99ae73d49aaa5f925a6ed7fb48cb87 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#rest> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#nil> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#causallyRelatedTo> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates one dimension being causally related with another in memes, e.g. text explaining why visual situation exists"@en .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#perspectiveAlignedWith> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#relatesTo> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#ObjectProperty> .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#modallyContradicting> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Indicates one dimension showing certainty versus another dimension showing ambiguity in memes, e.g. text certainty vs. visual ambiguity"@en .
